      "pins": [
        "U22.1",
        "U22.2",
        "U22.4",
        "U22.5"
      ],
      "nets": [
        "+3V3",
        "GND",
        "ESTOP_SINK1"
      ],
      "observed": "U22 is listed as a SN74LVC1G07 buffer. Its /OE pin is on ESTOP_SINK1, its A input is tied to GND, and its Y output is tied to the +3V3 power rail (the same net as its VCC). When /OE is pulled low, the buffer output pulls low and connects +3V3 to GND.",
      "expected": "The specification describes U22 as part of the e-stop sink/buffer chain feeding ESTOP_OUT, not as a switch that pulls the +3V3 supply rail to ground. U22's Y output should be connected to an e-stop signal node such as ESTOP_OUT or the common sink node, not to the +3V3 power net.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "+3V3 [Med Power] : ... U22.4(Y,output) U22.5(VCC,power_in)"
        },
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "GND [High Power] : ... U22.2(A,input) U22.3(GND,power_in)"
        },
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "ESTOP_SINK1 : J2.1(Pin_1,passive) R32.1(passive) SW7.8(passive) U22.1(/OE,input)"
        },
        {
          "source": "spec",
          "quote": "an e-stop loop with multiple sink points ... gated through single non-inverting buffers (SN74LVC1G07, parts U15, U22, U35, U36) wired as /OE-controlled sinks, feeding an ESTOP_OUT signal"
        }
      ],
      "impact": "Asserting ESTOP_SINK1, for example by closing SW7.8 or driving the J2.1 e-stop loop low, would short the +3V3 rail to ground through U22. This can brown out or overload the 3.3 V regulator and CM4 GPIO reference while failing to provide the intended e-stop sink action.",
      "confidence": 0.85
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The netlist shows U22's buffer output connected to the +3V3 supply rail with its input tied to ground, creating a conditional short of the 3.3 V rail when its e-stop /OE is asserted. This contradicts the specified e-stop sink/ESTOP_OUT connectivity and is a likely design or assembly anomaly."
}

